<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kyiv</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kiev</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kyiv is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> river. From the late 9th century it was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, the first East Slavic state, whose prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a> accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 988. Sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1240, it later came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and became the capital of independent Ukraine in 1991.</p></div>
